package dbcoverage

// Deterministic returns the DB dimension's deterministic coverage prose — the
// classes the DB rules affirm with certainty 1.0.
func Deterministic() []string {
	return []string{
		"Database structure — table without a primary key (DB-050): a model with no @id and no @@id. A table with no primary key is a structurally undeniable defect, so it is affirmed deterministically — BUT ONLY over a table whose structure the parser could PROVE complete (db.Table.Complete, ADR 0034, db-model-completeness-contract). When one or more statements affecting a table could not be reduced (a dropped ALTER TABLE shape, a malformed CREATE TABLE body, an unrecognized Prisma model-body line), DB-050 does NOT affirm — it ROUTES that table to a dedicated db-table-structure-unproven surface item instead (see Reasoning, below), carrying the raw unreduced statement and file:line so the agent can read the source DDL itself. \"Absence of DATA\" (a genuinely missing key) and \"absence of FEATURE\" (a key the parser could not read) are DIFFERENT claims, and DB-050 never blurs them. Read from the configured schema — a Prisma schema.prisma OR a directory of SQL-DDL (Flyway) migrations reconstructed to their final state (database.schema_paths). SQL-DDL parsing supports THREE dialects — PostgreSQL, MySQL, and SQL Server (T-SQL) — selected by the `database.type` setting in .codefit.yaml (postgresql | mysql | sqlserver); `sqlite` is recognized but returns an explicit \"not supported yet\" note rather than silently parsing as Postgres. All DB rules (DB-050 and below) are dialect-agnostic: they reason over the neutral db.Schema model reconstructed by whichever dialect parsed the DDL, never over dialect-specific fields, so the same rule set runs identically regardless of dialect. An unmapped type keyword in the source DDL (one the dialect's type vocabulary does not recognize) falls back to db.TypeUnknown — an honest fallback, never a silent guess. The DB dimension covers only what the schema states, not query behavior.",
	}
}

// Reasoning returns the DB dimension's coverage prose mapped as surface for the
// agent to reason over — schema-only structural facts codefit does not affirm.
func Reasoning() []string {
	return []string{
		"Database structure from the schema, mapped as surface for the agent to reason (read from database.schema_paths; schema-only, no query analysis): foreign keys with no covering index (DB-001) — a FK is covered when some index's leading columns match it (the primary key counts as an implicit index, a @unique as an index); whether an un-indexed FK matters depends on the table's size and access pattern, so codefit states the fact (fk_columns, existing_indexes, covering_index_detected=false) and the agent judges. Exact duplicate indexes (DB-011a) — two indexes on the same columns in the same order with the same uniqueness; which to drop is the human's call. Real-world coverage is dialect-uneven: on PostgreSQL/Pagila this rule fires on a GENUINE upstream duplicate (the payment_p2022_01 partition ships both idx_fk_payment_p2022_01_customer_id and payment_p2022_01_customer_id_idx on the identical column) — the only one of the three dialects where the positive case is proven by real vendored DDL rather than a constructed one; on MySQL/Sakila and SQL Server/AdventureWorks the rule is verified CLEAN against real DDL (no false positives), but its positive fire path is proven only by a constructed (synthetic) schema, since neither real corpus happens to ship a duplicate index. Multivalued (array) columns (DB-002) — an array violates 1NF, but a native array (Postgres) is legitimate sometimes, so it is surfaced not affirmed.",
		"Prefix-redundant indexes (DB-011b, Phase 2.2) — an index [a] that is a STRICT leading prefix of a wider index-like coverer [a,b] on the same table (a real index, or the primary key treated as an implicit index) is pure write/storage overhead, since the wider one already serves any lookup the narrower one would; which to drop is the human's call, so this is surface, never an affirmation. A UNIQUE index never fires (it enforces a uniqueness constraint the wider composite does not guarantee for its own leading columns alone). Supported on all three dialects — PostgreSQL/Pagila, MySQL/Sakila, and SQL Server/AdventureWorks all run CLEAN against their real vendored DDL (none of the three real corpora happens to contain a genuine prefix-redundant pair, a real honest finding about how these schemas are actually indexed, not a rule gap); the positive fire path on every dialect is proven by mutating a copy of a real table (e.g. adding a synthetic index on the leading column of Sakila's own real film_actor composite primary key [actor_id, film_id]), never by a fully synthetic fixture.",
		// PLACEMENT, stated rather than buried: the code×schema cross rules live in
		// internal/core/crossrules, not internal/core/dbrules, so their prose sits in
		// dbcoverage only because their output IS part of the DB dimension's — their
		// categories are unioned into the db baseline scope (ADR 0019/0029) and they are
		// reported in scan-all's DB section. Whether the cross deserves its own neutral
		// coverage leaf (a crosscoverage package, composed by append exactly as
		// dbcoverage is) remains an OPEN placement question. Appending here is reversible:
		// dbcoverage itself was relocated out of providers/typescript/coverage.go in 0.2.2.
		"Index-vs-query — the code's queries CROSSED with the schema (DB-010/DB-013, ADR 0029/0030/0031; scan-all only, Prisma only). Unlike every other DB entry here, these two do NOT reason over the schema alone: they read BOTH neutral inputs — the WHERE columns of the code's Prisma queries AND the schema's indexes — which is why they are a separate rule family with its own runner. Both are pure SURFACE, never affirmations: the structural fact (filtered-and-uncovered) is certain once the join matches, but whether a missing index MATTERS depends on the column's cardinality, the table's size and the write load, the same agent judgment DB-001 already defers. \"Covered\" is delegated to the SAME shared helpers of the neutral model that DB-001 and DW-010 use (db.IndexLike / db.CoveredByOrderedPrefix / db.CoveredBySetPrefix / db.UniqueKeys), so \"what serves a lookup\" is defined ONCE and cannot drift between rules: an index's LEADING columns count, the primary key counts as an implicit index, a @unique counts, and a filter that constrains a unique key is a single-row lookup that never fires. THE CERTAINTY GATE: the code↔schema join resolves a filter only when EXACTLY one table matches its model name and at least one filtered name is a real column of it — matching is by LOGICAL field name on both sides (a Prisma @map physical name never enters), exact and never fuzzy or case-insensitive, because all naming-convention knowledge (the Prisma Client accessor \"user\" → model \"User\") lives in the provider. Anything ambiguous or unmatched ABSTAINS and emits nothing — the Complete==false floor applied to the cross: a mutilated cross is worse than an absent one. (1) Filtered column with no index (DB-010): ONE column the code filters on that nothing — index, @unique, or primary key — covers as a LEADING column (a composite index [a,b] covers a lookup on a, not on b). Deduplicated per (table, column), so two queries filtering the same unindexed column are one concern reported once. A Boolean or enum column is deliberately SKIPPED: bounded by its declared type, where a standalone index is almost always wrong. The item anchors to the SCHEMA column — the filtering query is context, the fix is a schema change. (2) Multi-column filter with no composite index (DB-013): a WHERE a AND b with no composite index holding that column SET as its leading columns. Coverage here is ORDER-INSENSITIVE — [b,a] covers (a,b) — because codefit does not capture the WHERE operator and so cannot reason about equality-vs-range column order; it reports the set and leaves the order to the agent. It ABSTAINS at four or more filtered columns (which subset to index needs selectivity codefit cannot observe, so it says nothing rather than emitting a guess in an item's clothing). The same uncovered column set recurring across many models is GROUPED into ONE item naming every affected model — (salon_id, deleted_at) across fifteen models is one architectural observation (tenant scoping plus soft delete), not fifteen findings — anchored on the first model alphabetically, with the column set carried as the item's snippet so the baseline fingerprint stays stable no matter which model anchors it. PRECEDENCE, by construction: the two rules partition every resolved filter by its real column count — DB-010 owns the single-column ones, DB-013 the multi-column ones — so they never double-report one filter and neither inspects the other's output; a multi-column WHERE is deferred WHOLE to DB-013 rather than split into a standalone index per column. SCOPE: the code side needs a language provider that extracts query filters, and the TypeScript provider reading Prisma call-sites is the only one today; it reads the call's top-level where SPECIFICALLY, so a data/select/include/orderBy object whose keys look like columns is never mistaken for a filter, and a top-level AND is recursed and merged while OR/NOT are a declared, skipped limit. The cross runs inside scan-all only — the standalone codefit-scan-db tool is schema-only — and its categories are unioned into the db-dimension baseline scope so its items are tracked and pruned like every other db item. VOLUME scales with the schema, measured on dogfooded Prisma projects rather than assumed: roughly 7 items on 18 models, 21 on 40, 57 on 77 — a steady ~1/3 of the DB channel, proportional rather than runaway.",
		"Database structure — name-heuristic checks, mapped as surface (schema-only). These read meaning from column names, which is inherently noisy, so they are NEVER affirmed: codefit states the structural fact and the agent judges. (1) Foreign key typed as text (DB-051): a String/Text FK whose referenced key is numeric, or an unbounded @db.Text key — a String FK to a String uuid/cuid key does NOT fire (it is a structural type-mismatch check, not a name guess), and an unresolvable reference does not fire. Facts: type_mismatch, text_key, referenced_type_resolved. (2) Missing audit timestamps (DB-052): a table with NEITHER createdAt NOR updatedAt; looks_like_join_table is exposed so link tables can be dismissed. Only-one-missing is a deferred candidate, not fired yet. (3) Sensitive column stored in the clear (DB-053): a column whose name matches a sensitive token (password, token, apiKey, ssn, …) and whose type holds a value (String/Text/Bytes). It ALWAYS emits; an encryption hint in the name (passwordHash, encrypted…) is reported as the fact encryption_hint_in_name, NOT used to suppress — a name is not a guarantee, and hiding a possible plaintext secret would be a silent false negative. Names are matched by component (camelCase/snake_case), never raw substring, so passwordChangedAt (a DateTime) and passwordResetCount (an Int) do not fire. (4) Repeating groups (DB-003): two or more same-typed columns sharing a base name with numeric suffixes (phone1/phone2/phone3) — a 1NF smell the agent weighs against an intentional fixed set (address line 1/2).",
		"View sensitive-column exposure (DB-020, Phase 2.2) — a VIEW whose top-level SELECT column list exposes a column or alias whose name matches a sensitive token (the same vocabulary DB-053 uses). Reads the view's captured body text through a deliberately bounded SELECT-projection scanner, never a general SQL-expression parser: a function call, CASE expression, or subquery item with no alias is a declared miss for that one item, and SELECT * is a declared miss for the whole view (the column list is not literal in the DDL text). Never an affirmation — a column named password in a view says nothing about whether the underlying value is masked, hashed, or genuinely exposed to whoever queries the view, so this is always surface. Runs CLEAN on real DDL across all three dialects: Pagila's actor_info, AdventureWorks' vEmployee, and Sakila's customer_list all legitimately confirm the negative case (a real, well-designed view's whole purpose is usually a curated projection, so it rarely re-exposes a raw sensitive column under a sensitive-looking name — a real finding about how views are written, not a gap in the rule). The POSITIVE fire path is proven ONLY by a CONSTRUCTED case: renaming the real, vendored Sakila customer_list view's own trailing \"AS SID\" alias to \"AS ssn\". No real view in any of the three dogfooded corpora genuinely exposes a sensitive-named column — that is stated plainly, not implied by the negative runs above. Zero value on Prisma-only projects: Prisma's schema.prisma has no view-block concept (ADR 0014 places it out of scope), so a Prisma-only project has no views for this rule to read at all.",
		"Routine without exception handling (DB-031, 0.2.3 routine-body rules) — a stored procedure OR function (both surface as db.Procedure) whose captured body contains NO exception-handling construct for its dialect: T-SQL BEGIN TRY (paired with BEGIN CATCH), MySQL DECLARE ... HANDLER, or PL/pgSQL EXCEPTION WHEN. This states an ABSENCE as a structural fact, NEVER an affirmation of a defect: whether the missing handler matters is the agent's judgment, and so is the ADEQUACY of a handler that IS present — an empty CATCH, or a handler that swallows every error, reads as \"present\" here (the construct exists) yet may still be a real bug. DB-031 detects the STRUCTURE, it does not grade it. Read through a deliberately bounded, string/comment-aware token scanner (the same declared-subset discipline as DB-020), never a general SQL parser: a handler-shaped token inside a string literal or a comment does not false-match. CRITICAL: PostgreSQL RAISE EXCEPTION is a THROW, not a handler — the scanner matches EXCEPTION only when the very next keyword token is WHEN, so RAISE EXCEPTION and a bare EXCEPTION never count as handling. Gated on Body.Complete (ADR 0004/0025): a body the parser could not prove whole is never evaluated, so an absence over unread/truncated text is never falsely affirmed. Per-dialect coverage against REAL vendored DDL: MySQL/Sakila — real POSITIVE (rewards_report, no HANDLER) and real NEGATIVE (inventory_held_by_customer, real DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND); T-SQL/AdventureWorks — real POSITIVE (uspGetBillOfMaterials, a recursive-CTE body with no TRY/CATCH) and real NEGATIVE (uspUpdateEmployeePersonalInfo, real BEGIN TRY ... BEGIN CATCH); PostgreSQL — real POSITIVE (Pagila rewards_report, which RAISEs EXCEPTION twice but has NO EXCEPTION WHEN — the bare-EXCEPTION trap, locked by an explicit test) plus a CONSTRUCTED NEGATIVE, declared synthetic in testdata/pg_constructed_exception_handler.sql (a small hand-written safe_divide function with a real BEGIN ... EXCEPTION WHEN ... END block), since no routine in the dogfooded Pagila excerpt contains a real handler clause. TRIGGERS are intentionally out of scope for DB-031 (their cross-table/external-effect risks are DB-040/DB-041's domain). Zero value on Prisma-only projects: Prisma's schema.prisma has no stored-procedure/function block concept, so a Prisma-only project has no routines for this rule to read.",
		"Trigger cross-table cascade (DB-040, 0.2.3 routine-body rules) — a TRIGGER whose body performs DML (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) against a table OTHER than the one it fires on. Surface, never an affirmation: it states the structural facts (writes_other_table: X for each other table, and documented_by_comment) and the AGENT judges whether the cascade is intentional and correct — DB-040 detects the cross-table write, it does not grade whether the cascade is right. Body source is per-dialect (ADR 0026): MySQL/T-SQL triggers carry an INLINE body scanned directly; a PostgreSQL trigger has NO inline body, so the rule resolves Schema.ExecutedProcedure(trigger) to the executed function and scans ITS body, still comparing writes against the trigger's own table — an unresolvable built-in (e.g. tsvector_update_trigger) makes the rule abstain honestly (it cannot see the logic). Bounded string/comment-aware scanner (the DB-020/DB-031 declared-subset discipline), never a SQL-expression parser: a DML-shaped token inside a string literal or comment does not fabricate a write; the T-SQL UPDATE(column) column-changed function is excluded (it names no table); the trigger's own event clause (AFTER UPDATE, INSTEAD OF DELETE, INSERT OR UPDATE) is not mistaken for a statement; and a schema-qualified write to the trigger's OWN table (\"Purchasing\".\"PurchaseOrderDetail\" on a trigger for PurchaseOrderDetail) is correctly NOT a cascade. Gated on Body.Complete (ADR 0004/0025): a body the parser could not prove whole is never evaluated. Per-dialect coverage: T-SQL/AdventureWorks — real POSITIVE (uPurchaseOrderDetail cascades into TransactionHistory and PurchaseOrderHeader, while its same-table PurchaseOrderDetail UPDATE is correctly excluded) and real NEGATIVE (dEmployee, an INSTEAD OF DELETE that only RAISERRORs and ROLLBACKs); MySQL/Sakila — real POSITIVE (ins_film/upd_film/del_film each cascade a write into film_text) and a CONSTRUCTED NEGATIVE declared synthetic in testdata/mysql/constructed_non_cascading_trigger.sql (a trigger that only stamps NEW.created_at), since every real Sakila trigger cascades; PostgreSQL — real NEGATIVE (Pagila last_updated, whose function only sets NEW.last_update) and a CONSTRUCTED POSITIVE declared synthetic in testdata/pg_constructed_cascade_trigger.sql (a trigger→function pair whose function INSERTs into an order_audit table, exercising the ADR-0026 resolution path), since no real Pagila trigger writes another table. Zero value on Prisma-only projects: Prisma's schema.prisma has no trigger block concept.",
		"Trigger external-effecting call (DB-041, 0.2.3 routine-body rules) — a TRIGGER whose body invokes a call that reaches OUTSIDE the database (a shell exec, OLE automation, email, a remote/cross-database query, an async notification, or a pipe to a program). Surface, never an affirmation: it states the fact (external_call: X) and the AGENT judges whether the call is safe — DB-041 detects the external call, it does not decide whether it is exploitable. STRICT vocabulary is the line between real risk and noise: an EXECUTE/CALL of an INTERNAL stored procedure (EXECUTE dbo.uspLogError, CALL recompute_totals) is a normal routine call and does NOT fire — that is the rule's trap, the token that looks like a call but is not external. Per-dialect vocabulary: T-SQL (xp_cmdshell, sp_OA* OLE automation, sp_send_dbmail, OPENROWSET, OPENQUERY); PostgreSQL (dblink/dblink_exec, NOTIFY/pg_notify, COPY ... PROGRAM); MySQL (sys_exec/sys_eval UDFs). Body source is per-dialect, resolving a PostgreSQL trigger to its executed function (ADR 0026) exactly as DB-040 does; gated on Body.Complete. Bounded string/comment-aware scanner: an external-call token inside a string literal or comment does not fire, and a member access (NEW.notify) is not mistaken for the NOTIFY statement. Per-dialect coverage: T-SQL/AdventureWorks — a CONSTRUCTED POSITIVE declared synthetic in testdata/tsql/constructed_external_call_trigger.sql (a trigger that EXECs xp_cmdshell) and a real NEGATIVE / trap (uPurchaseOrderDetail, whose only calls are EXECUTE of the INTERNAL uspPrintError/uspLogError logging procs — not external); PostgreSQL — a CONSTRUCTED POSITIVE declared synthetic in testdata/pg_constructed_external_call_trigger.sql (a trigger→function pair whose function issues NOTIFY, exercising the ADR-0026 resolution path) and a real NEGATIVE (Pagila last_updated); MySQL — DETECTABLE WITHOUT DOGFOOD: the scanner recognizes the MySQL external vocabulary (sys_exec/sys_eval) and WOULD fire if one appeared, but a trigger making an external call is structurally rare and non-idiomatic in MySQL (no NOTIFY, external effects need exotic UDFs), so no real or constructed MySQL case is dogfooded. This is DISTINCT from NotCovered (which would mean non-detectable): the capability exists, only the dogfood evidence is absent by the dialect's nature — stated plainly rather than faked with an unrealistic constructed case. Zero value on Prisma-only projects: Prisma's schema.prisma has no trigger block concept.",
		"Dynamic SQL construction in a routine (DB-030, 0.2.3 routine-body rules) — a stored procedure OR function (both surface as db.Procedure) whose body BUILDS and runs SQL from a string at runtime. Surface, never an affirmation: it states the fact that dynamic SQL is constructed (dynamic_sql: <marker>) and the AGENT judges whether it is injectable — codefit maps the surface, it deliberately does NOT do taint analysis (whether the string mixes in untrusted input is the agent's reasoning over the surface). THE TRAP: a static EXEC/CALL of a NAMED internal procedure (EXEC dbo.uspFoo, CALL recompute_totals) is NOT dynamic SQL and does not fire — the same EXEC family as DB-041's trap, excluded for a different reason (there 'not external', here 'not dynamic'; an EXEC dbo.uspLogError fires neither). Per-dialect markers: T-SQL (sp_executesql; EXEC(<expr>)/EXECUTE(<expr>) in parentheses, NOT a literal proc name); PL/pgSQL (EXECUTE '<string>' / EXECUTE format(...); quote_literal / quote_ident string building); MySQL (PREPARE ... FROM a built string). Bounded string/comment-aware scanner; gated on Body.Complete (ADR 0004/0025). Declared limit: a bare EXECUTE <variable> with no construction marker visible in the same body is a miss (the string may have been built out of view) — a known gap, not a silent one. Per-dialect coverage: PostgreSQL — real POSITIVE (Pagila rewards_report builds a query string with quote_literal and || concatenation and runs it via EXECUTE) and real NEGATIVE (last_updated); MySQL — a CONSTRUCTED POSITIVE declared synthetic in testdata/mysql/constructed_dynamic_sql_proc.sql (PREPARE ... FROM a CONCATenated string) and a real NEGATIVE (Sakila rewards_report, temp-table based, no dynamic SQL); T-SQL — a CONSTRUCTED POSITIVE declared synthetic in testdata/tsql/constructed_dynamic_sql_proc.sql (sp_executesql over a built string) and a real NEGATIVE (uspGetBillOfMaterials, a recursive CTE). Zero value on Prisma-only projects: Prisma's schema.prisma has no stored-procedure/function block concept.",
		"Star-schema and slowly-changing-dimension checks (DW-001/002/005/010/011, S2 of the RF-03 OLAP closure) — five rules that read the schema PLUS the S1 paradigm/table-role classification, and that reach ONLY tables classified fact- or dimension-role; an oltp or unclassified table is never evaluated by any of them. All five are pure SURFACE, never affirmations (ADR 0017): a warehouse-modelling choice is a design judgment, not a structurally undeniable defect, so codefit states the observed shape and the agent decides. (1) Fact table with no dimension foreign key (DW-001): a fact-role table whose foreign keys reach no dimension-role table. A foreign key to another FACT, to staging, or to an unclassified table deliberately does NOT count — a fact-to-fact bridge looks joined but carries no dimensional context. A fact with zero foreign keys fires too, and says so (foreign_keys: (none)) rather than silently passing on an empty list. (2) Dimension without a surrogate key (DW-002): a dimension-role table whose primary key is composite, or a single column that is not provably an integer surrogate. The surrogate test is structural and narrow on purpose — a one-column primary key of integer type, the shape every well-modelled warehouse uses — so no name guessing is involved. DECLARED LIMIT (a): a UUID/GUID surrogate types as a string in the neutral model and therefore FIRES; the emitted facts (composite_primary_key, integer_primary_key, primary_key_column_resolved) are what the agent needs to dismiss it in one step. DECLARED LIMIT (b), the incomplete-reconstruction path: when the primary key names a SINGLE column that the parser did not reconstruct onto the table, the surrogate test cannot be satisfied — proving an integer surrogate requires reading the column's type — so the rule FIRES as not-provably-a-surrogate and names the reason rather than hiding it: primary_key_column_resolved=false, with the primary_key_type signal reading \"(column not declared in the parsed schema)\". This is deliberate and test-locked, and it is the OPPOSITE choice from DB-051, where an unresolvable reference does NOT fire: DB-051 compares a FK's type against the referenced key's type and has nothing to compare when the reference does not resolve, whereas DW-002 asks whether a surrogate is PROVEN, and an unread column proves nothing. The state is reachable, not hypothetical: SQL-DDL known limit (5) below drops a column whose name is an index keyword and whose type is outside the dialect's vocabulary (Pagila's real film.fulltext), while a table-level PRIMARY KEY (fulltext) naming it survives into the model. A dimension with NO primary key at all ABSTAINS — DB-050 already affirms that case deterministically, and two IDs for one defect is noise. (3) Facts present, no time dimension (DW-005): schema-level, at most ONE item anchored on the first fact table. A time dimension is recognized by EITHER the conventional name (dim_date/dim_time/dim_calendar, separator-insensitive) OR the structural grain — a dimension whose primary key is a single date/datetime column, which is date-grained by construction. The structural signal keys on the PRIMARY KEY, not on \"contains a date column\": an updated_at audit stamp is not a calendar, and accepting any date column would suppress the rule on almost every schema — a silent false negative. DECLARED LIMIT: a calendar keyed by an integer yyyymmdd smart key (AdventureWorksDW's own DimDate) is recognized only by NAME, because that key is structurally indistinguishable from any other surrogate. (4) SCD-2 dimension without a currency index (DW-010): a dimension carrying slowly-changing columns (valid_from/valid_to/is_current/effective_date, separator-insensitive so validTo/isCurrent are matched too) where no index leads with valid_to or is_current — so every \"give me the current version\" query scans the whole version history, the part that grows without bound. Index coverage is delegated to the SAME shared helpers DB-001 and DB-010 use (db.IndexLike / db.CoveredByOrderedPrefix), so \"what serves a lookup\" is defined once in the neutral model: the primary key counts as an implicit index, and a composite index NOT leading on the currency column does not cover it. EITHER currency column being covered is enough to go quiet. A dimension with no slowly-changing columns is never evaluated; one with history columns but NEITHER currency column abstains rather than demanding an index no query would use. (5) Mixed SCD strategies (DW-011): schema-level, one item, when some dimensions keep history and others overwrite in place — a report joining both mixes point-in-time with as-of-today attributes. TIME DIMENSIONS ARE EXCLUDED from the comparison (a calendar is not slowly-changing by definition), because counting one as SCD-1 would fire this rule on essentially every correctly built warehouse; a genuine split between two other dimensions still fires. DW-010 and DW-011 share one history vocabulary so they cannot disagree about what \"keeps history\" means, and share its declared limit: a dimension using a different vocabulary (AdventureWorksDW's DimProduct uses StartDate/EndDate/Status) reads as SCD-1. DOGFOOD STATUS, stated plainly rather than implied: the positive and trap fire paths of all five rules are proven by CONSTRUCTED (declared synthetic, ADR 0028) schemas, not by real vendored DDL. Microsoft's AdventureWorksDW IS vendored (testdata/tsql/adventureworksdw_real_objects.sql, MIT) and yields NO DW finding, for two independent test-locked reasons: its PascalCase Kimball names (FactInternetSales, DimCustomer) are outside the recognized snake_case prefix vocabulary, and the T-SQL reducer drops the ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT shapes it uses, so its real primary and foreign keys never reach the model at all (see the SQL-DDL known limits in NotCovered). ZERO VALUE ON PRISMA: a schema.prisma expresses no warehouse concept, so these rules can only classify a Prisma project whose models happen to be named fact_/dim_.",
		"Table structural completeness (db-model-completeness-contract, ADR 0034) — every table carries a completeness signal (db.Table.Complete/Note/Unreduced) mirroring the pre-existing Body.Complete idiom (ADR 0004/0025) at TABLE granularity: false means the parser met at least one statement affecting the table it could NOT reduce and could not rule out as declaring a key/index/column. A DECLARED, RECOGNIZED skip (CHECK/EXCLUDE/PARTITION constraints; ALTER COLUMN/RENAME/OWNER/ENABLE/DISABLE/CLUSTER/SET/RESET/VALIDATE/NO alter actions) is NOT incompleteness — recording those would mute the whole DB dimension on ordinary PostgreSQL DDL, so only a GENUINELY unrecognized statement demotes a table. On the Prisma path, a model-body line that is neither a recognized field nor a @@-attribute demotes the table the same way; the deferred, unrelated top-level-line skip (a view block) does not. Two-way disposition, no rule signature change (ADR 0015): DB-050 (the dimension's one AFFIRMATION) ROUTES an unproven table to the db-table-structure-unproven surface category instead of affirming; DB-001, DB-052, and the five DW-0xx rules ABSTAIN silently on an unproven table (a dropped statement might have declared the very index/column/key each rule is asking about). DW-005 and DW-011 are SCHEMA-LEVEL census judgments and abstain the WHOLE rule — never a per-table skip, which would silently shrink the census and still emit. A genuinely PK-less table with a PROVEN-complete structure still affirms DB-050 at confidence 1.0 — honesty costs nothing here. Per-scan, sensors/db.Result.Note (reaching the agent through scan-all's DBSection.Note) carries a bounded INVENTORY of what could not be measured, aggregated by REASON never by table (a 200-table systematic gap is one line, not 200) — this is an inventory of WHAT was measured, never a DIAGNOSIS of WHY the parser failed (no parser-internal identifiers reach scan output; the closed Reason* vocabulary in core/db is the type-level control). BOUNDARY, stated so this contract does not over-promise: Complete covers DROPS, not FABRICATIONS — a reducer that believes it succeeded while inventing data (Pagila's film.fulltext phantom index, and the closed \"ADD  CONSTRAINT\" double-space fabrication, both documented in the SQL-DDL known limits below) reports Complete=true regardless; that class needs its own, separate control.",
		"Index access method (db.Index.Method, index-method-capture) — every index the schema-parsing providers read now carries its DECLARED access method/kind when the source states one, lowercased at every capture site for one convention across dialects: PostgreSQL's USING <method> on a standalone CREATE INDEX, before the column list (CREATE INDEX ix ON t USING gin (col)); MySQL's USING BTREE|HASH on a standalone CREATE INDEX, the DIFFERENT post-column-list position (CREATE INDEX ix ON t (col) USING BTREE|HASH); the SAME MySQL USING clause on INLINE and ALTER TABLE ADD table-constraint forms too — UNIQUE/KEY/INDEX/FULLTEXT/SPATIAL (...) USING BTREE|HASH, in EITHER grammar position (before or after the column list), at a DIFFERENT call site (applyTableConstraint) than the standalone statement, via the shared indexMethodOutsideParens helper; T-SQL's CLUSTERED/NONCLUSTERED ordinary-index kind; and T-SQL's CREATE [CLUSTERED] COLUMNSTORE INDEX kind, parsed by its OWN dedicated regex (reCreateColumnstoreIndex — a genuinely DIFFERENT statement shape, not reCreateIndex widened, since it carries no column list at all) and captured unconditionally as \"columnstore\" (the CLUSTERED qualifier is that statement's own default when omitted, so it carries no extra information beyond \"this is a columnstore index\") — its Columns is left EMPTY, never synthesized, because that statement names no column in its own grammar (it implicitly covers every column) and inventing one would misrepresent what the source actually said. Prisma's @@index(..., type: X) argument is captured the same way — DELIBERATELY not validated against any codefit-maintained vocabulary, since Prisma's own accepted set of index types has not been verified against every provider this project supports; whatever the schema text declares is captured verbatim, only lowercased. EMPTY means \"no access method declared in source\" — the same empty-means-none convention as Table.DBName — and is NEVER defaulted to a guessed value like \"btree\". This closes THREE structurally different classes of parser blindness (architecture/two-classes-of-parser-blindness, obs #1307), stated separately rather than collapsed into one: (1) DISCARD — PostgreSQL's anonymous index and T-SQL's ordinary CLUSTERED/NONCLUSTERED index were previously DROPPED entirely by reCreateIndex's grammar (T-SQL's CLUSTERED COLUMNSTORE INDEX was ALSO dropped, by a DIFFERENT statement shape reCreateIndex never covered at all) — the completeness contract (ADR 0034) already covered this honestly by marking the table unproven; this slice teaches the parser to actually READ these forms, so the abstention DISAPPEARS rather than staying reported. (2) OMISSION at the standalone CREATE INDEX call site — MySQL's post-column-list USING and Prisma's type: argument: the statement parsed successfully, only the value was silently discarded; closed by actually extracting it. (3) OMISSION at the INLINE/ALTER TABLE constraint call site (applyTableConstraint) — the SAME MySQL USING defect one level down, in a DIFFERENT function than (2), independently closed by the same extraction logic applied at that call site too. DECLARED BOUNDARY, honest abstention rather than a new capability: a PostgreSQL EXPRESSION index (CREATE INDEX ON t (lower(email))) is explicitly OUT OF SCOPE — parsing SQL expressions is not attempted. Making the anonymous-index name optional widened reCreateIndex's reach enough to also MATCH an expression index's outer parens; without a guard, its naive \\(([^)]*)\\) column-list grammar would truncate at the FIRST nested ')' and FABRICATE a phantom column literally named the truncated expression text, a regression from honest abstention to silent fabrication caught before release. The fix verifies the column-list span against balancedParen (the SAME helper applyCreateTable already uses); a mismatch routes the statement to the SAME floor a genuinely unrecognized CREATE INDEX-shaped statement uses (markUnrecognizedIndexShape), never silently proven complete. See the SQL-DDL known limits below (item 7) for the narrower set of CREATE INDEX-family shapes still genuinely unread after this slice.",
		"Paradigm and table-role detection, plus 3NF-suppression on OLAP-classified tables (S1, RF-03 OLAP closure) — codefit computes a schema's paradigm (oltp | olap | mixed) and each table's warehouse role (fact | dimension | staging | mart | unclassified) as a pure function of the schema: table-name prefixes (fact_/dim_/stg_/mart_) are the PRIMARY signal, corroborated by REAL relational structure — fact_ tables need foreign-key fan-out to 2+ distinct tables, dim_ tables need to be REFERENCED (fan-in) by at least one other table. A lone single-column surrogate primary key is deliberately NOT, by itself, sufficient corroboration (nearly every ordinary OLTP table has one, so it would classify almost anything prefixed fact_/dim_ as warehouse-role, fixed post-review — see ADR 0033 decision 2). stg_/mart_ still need no structural signal in S1. `database.paradigm` defaults to \"auto\" (detection decides); an explicit oltp/olap/mixed value in .codefit.yaml ALWAYS wins over detection — developer autonomy is innegotiable. Detection alone never mutates a finding; it feeds ONE first consumer this slice: DB-002 (multivalued column) and DB-003 (repeating groups) — both STILL schema-only, deterministic 1NF checks in dbrules, unchanged in shape — are SUPPRESSED by a sensor-level pass for fact/dimension/mart-role tables under auto detection or an explicit mixed override (an intentionally denormalized warehouse table is not a 1NF violation, PRD line 382), or dropped SCHEMA-WIDE under an explicit olap override (the developer asserts the whole schema is a warehouse). An explicit oltp override NEVER suppresses, regardless of any detected role — the same identical repeating-group/multivalued shape still fires exactly as before this slice. Suppression is never silent: when items are withheld, the sensor's Result.Note carries a factual trace of how many and on how many tables, plus the database.paradigm: oltp escape hatch to see them (developer autonomy is always informed of the consequence). This was the FIRST OLAP-aware behavior. The star-schema/slowly-changing-dimension half of the DW-0xx family landed in S2 and IS covered — DW-001/002/005/010/011, see the entry above. The columnar/analytic-index check (DW-021) landed in S3 — see the next entry, below. What remains NOT built is the partitioning check (DW-020, planned for S4); see NotCovered.",
		"Fact table missing a columnar/analytic index (DW-021, S3 of the RF-03 OLAP closure) — a fact-role table with no index using a RECOGNIZED columnar/analytic access method. Pure SURFACE, never an affirmation (ADR 0017): whether the absence matters depends on this table's real size and query pattern, which codefit cannot see from static DDL — a small fact table, or one queried only by primary key, has no use for a columnar index. VOCABULARY, defined in exactly ONE place (dwrules.columnarIndexMethods) so extending it is a vocabulary change that never touches control flow: PostgreSQL contributes brin and gin; T-SQL contributes columnstore, captured verbatim by the parser as of index-method-capture (PR #79, db.Index.Method). MySQL contributes nothing — its only index methods, btree and hash, are ordinary row-store methods, not columnar ones, so there is no MySQL-specific branch anywhere in this rule. GATING, per table, on db.Table.StructureProven() (ADR 0034/db-model-completeness-contract) — the SAME pattern DW-001 already uses, not DW-005/DW-011's whole-rule abstain, because DW-021 is a per-table question: one fact table's dropped statement has no bearing on any other table's answer. This is the entire mechanism that makes the rule dialect-agnostic: a statement that could have declared the very columnar index this rule asks about — PostgreSQL's ON ONLY clause on a partitioned parent table's own index, the standalone CREATE FULLTEXT/SPATIAL/XML/PRIMARY XML INDEX forms, T-SQL's CREATE NONCLUSTERED COLUMNSTORE INDEX (which, unlike its CLUSTERED counterpart, carries an explicit column list), and MySQL's fourth USING position (between the index name and ON) — already marks its table Complete=false via the completeness contract's existing machinery (see the SQL-DDL known limits below, item 7), so this rule's single StructureProven() gate abstains automatically on every one of those forms, with NO per-dialect branch in dw021.go itself. This closes the S3 attempt this project shipped and then FROZE at review (sdd/db-olap-rules/s3-4r-ledger-frozen): the frozen version answered from blindness on every input path because the parser could not read index access methods at all; index-method-capture fixed the parser floor first, and this rule is deliberately nothing more than reading what the parser now provides. PRISMA IS NOT ZERO-VALUE, unlike the sibling DW-001/002/005/010/011 family (whose blanket Prisma-zero-value note concerns partitioning/materialized-view/warehouse-modelling concepts schema.prisma has no syntax for at all): Prisma's own @@index([...], type: X) syntax (the extendedIndexes preview feature) DOES let a schema declare a columnar/analytic method, and the Prisma provider captures it verbatim (lowercased) exactly like every other db.Index.Method call site — a fact-role Prisma model with @@index([col], type: Brin) genuinely suppresses this rule, proven end to end through the real Prisma provider (TestSensorDB_DW021_RealPrismaProvider). What a Prisma-only project still cannot get from this rule: T-SQL's columnstore vocabulary word has no Prisma equivalent, so a Prisma project can only ever satisfy DW-021 via brin/gin. DOGFOOD STATUS, stated plainly: the positive and negative/trap fire paths are proven THROUGH THE REAL PARSER, not a hand-built db.Index literal — real PostgreSQL DDL parsed by sqlddl.New() for the plain-index positive and the USING brin negative, real T-SQL DDL parsed under the SQLServer dialect for the CREATE CLUSTERED COLUMNSTORE INDEX negative, and the real Prisma provider for both the no-type: positive and the type: Brin negative — plus an end-to-end abstention proof against a REAL genuinely-unrecognized shape (PostgreSQL's ON ONLY, the same shape internal/providers/sqlddl/testdata/pg_constructed_unrecognized_index_forms.sql already vendors) confirming the table lands StructureProven()=false and DW-021 emits nothing for it. No real vendored warehouse corpus is used for DW-021 specifically — the same AdventureWorksDW naming/ALTER-CONSTRAINT limits that keep the whole DW-0xx family blind to that corpus (see the S2 entry above) apply here too.",
	}
}

// NotCovered returns the DB dimension's explicit gaps: what codefit does not
// audit for this dimension, stated plainly rather than left silent.
func NotCovered() []string {
	return []string{
		"Index-vs-query analysis — whether the schema indexes the columns the application code actually filters on — IS now covered, by DB-010 and DB-013 (see the code×schema cross entry in Reasoning, above), for Prisma projects, inside scan-all. What remains NOT covered is the narrower set of cases the cross itself declares and abstains on: (1) RANGE vs EQUALITY — the neutral query model carries no WHERE operator at all, so a range predicate (gt/lt/contains/startsWith) is indistinguishable from an equality one; DB-013 therefore treats the filtered columns as an unordered SET and never advises on index column ORDER, which is a false-negative direction, never a wrong suggestion. (2) A column whose DECLARED type does not reveal its real cardinality — DB-010 skips only a Boolean or an enum, the two types bounded by construction, so a String used as an enum (income/expense, a role) or a nullable DateTime used as a binary flag (deletedAt: null = live) still emits and the agent refutes it by reading the schema; resolving it needs the query model to carry the WHERE's literal VALUES, a separate slice. (3) CROSS-NAMING-SPACE — the code↔schema join matches logical names EXACTLY, so a Prisma field name crossed against a physically named SQL-DDL column abstains and emits nothing (a mutilated cross is worse than an absent one). (4) CROSS-TABLE (join) filters — one query filter names ONE model, and a nested relation-filter key is dropped when the join to the schema resolves, so nothing is ever checked about a joined table's indexes. Also abstained by design: an OR/NOT clause is skipped at extraction (it does not reduce to one indexable column set), and DB-013 stays silent at four or more filtered columns (which subset to index needs selectivity codefit cannot see). SCOPE, stated plainly rather than implied: the cross needs a language provider that extracts query filters, and TypeScript/Prisma is the only one today — a Go project, or any non-Prisma data-access layer, yields no filters and therefore no cross. It runs in scan-all only; the standalone codefit-scan-db tool is schema-only and never crosses code. Whether an existing index is ACTUALLY USED at runtime is a different, telemetry-only question — see the DB-012 entry below. (N+1 query-in-loop patterns are a DIFFERENT capability again: N+1 is mapped as per-handler surface by the language provider, not by the DB dimension, so it appears in scan-all's endpoint buckets, never in this DB section.)",
		"Never-used index (DB-012) is NOT covered, and this is PERMANENT, not deferred: detecting an unused index requires runtime query telemetry (e.g. PostgreSQL's pg_stat_user_indexes) that only exists inside a live, running database with real traffic history. codefit's model is static and never connects to a database — it reads only DDL/schema text — so this rule is structurally incompatible with how codefit operates, not merely unscheduled.",
		"The routine-body rule family — DB-030 (dynamic SQL construction), DB-031 (routine without exception handling), DB-040 (trigger cross-table cascade), and DB-041 (trigger external-effecting call) — is now COMPLETE: all four are covered as surface (see Reasoning, above), none deferred. The parser prerequisite is DONE — a multi-statement T-SQL routine body is captured COMPLETE to the GO batch separator (or EOF), NOT truncated at its first internal ';' (ADR 0027; PostgreSQL dollar-quoted and MySQL DELIMITER-wrapped bodies were already complete) — which is what let these rules read whole T-SQL bodies safely; each still gates on Body.Complete so a body the parser could not prove whole is never evaluated (never a false affirmation over unread text). (Had DB-031 shipped over the OLD truncated T-SQL body, \"is exception handling present?\" would have FALSELY AFFIRMED an absence that was really just unread text past the cut — which is why the parser fix came first.) The whole family carries the same Prisma-zero-value limit as DB-020: Prisma's schema.prisma has no stored-procedure/trigger block concept, so a Prisma-only project gets no value from any of these rules.",
		"OLAP / data-warehouse RULE FAMILY (DW-0xx, NARROWED AGAIN as of S3): paradigm/table-role detection, 3NF-suppression on OLAP-classified tables, the star-schema/slowly-changing-dimension checks (DW-001/002/005/010/011), AND the columnar/analytic-index check (DW-021) — ARE now covered (see Reasoning, above). What remains NOT covered: a partitioning check (DW-020, planned for S4) of this same RF-03 OLAP-closure change. It does not fire today, under any dialect. Materialized-view refresh staleness (a DW-022 candidate) was evaluated and permanently DROPPED, same DB-012 lineage as never-used-index: refresh cadence lives in external cron/scheduler state absent from static DDL, so it is structurally incompatible with codefit's static model, not merely unscheduled. Separately from the rules: table-role detection recognizes only the snake_case prefixes fact_/dim_/stg_/mart_, so a warehouse using PascalCase Kimball naming (FactInternetSales, DimCustomer — Microsoft's own AdventureWorksDW does exactly this) classifies entirely as unclassified and gets NO value from any DW rule, DW-021 included. That is a naming-vocabulary limit, not a rule gap, and it is locked as a test against the vendored AdventureWorksDW DDL rather than left silent.",
		"SQL-DDL dialect known limits (declared, not silent): (1) a T-SQL routine body is captured to the GO batch separator (or EOF), so a CREATE TABLE-shaped fragment inside a GO-batched procedure/trigger body is absorbed into the body, NOT surfaced as a spurious top-level table (ADR 0027, closing a limit ADR 0022 had declared); the trade is that a T-SQL routine with NO trailing GO immediately followed by another statement absorbs that statement into the body — invalid T-SQL batching, the intentional boundary of ADR 0027. MySQL routine bodies wrapped in DELIMITER //...// are unaffected. (2) A MySQL client DELIMITER directive is recognized only when its argument is punctuation (//, $$); a word-based delimiter such as DELIMITER GO is NOT recognized. (3) The T-SQL GO batch separator is recognized only when a line is exactly \"GO\"; a column literally named go alone on its own line would collide (vanishingly rare, accepted). (4) An inline index whose NAME is itself a type keyword (e.g. KEY int (col), an index named \"int\") is read as a column — the KEY/INDEX-vs-column discriminator trusts a type-named token as a column (pathological, accepted). (5) A column named exactly key, index, fulltext, or spatial whose type is NOT in the dialect's recognized type vocabulary (e.g. PostgreSQL's tsvector, as in real Pagila's film.fulltext column) collides with the SAME inline-index-shorthand heuristic from a different direction: the column is silently dropped and a phantom zero-column index is fabricated in its place instead. Confirmed against real vendored Pagila DDL; not yet fixed. This is a FABRICATION, not a silent drop, so db.Table.Complete (ADR 0034) cannot catch it: the reducer believes it succeeded. The completeness contract's own doc comment states this boundary explicitly (\"Complete covers DROPS, not FABRICATIONS\") rather than over-promising a guarantee this mechanism does not provide. (6) THREE shapes of ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT are dropped by the reducer, so the keys they declare never reach the model: T-SQL's WITH CHECK / WITH NOCHECK prefix (ALTER TABLE x WITH CHECK ADD CONSTRAINT ...); any whitespace other than a single space between ADD and CONSTRAINT (a newline, as formatted DDL commonly writes it); and comma-chained constraint lists (ADD CONSTRAINT a ..., CONSTRAINT b ...), where every constraint after the first is lost because it does not repeat ADD. Confirmed against real vendored AdventureWorksDW DDL, which uses ALL THREE — its three real primary keys and all eight real foreign keys are invisible. These three SHAPES are still NOT parsed (deliberately deferred parser-shape debt, unchanged by db-model-completeness-contract, 2026-07-30) — but the DROP is no longer SILENT: each now marks the table's structural completeness (db.Table.Complete=false, ADR 0034) and DB-050 ROUTES the table to a db-table-structure-unproven surface item (the raw unreduced statement plus file:line) instead of affirming. Real vendored AdventureWorksDW now yields ZERO DB-050 false affirmations and exactly 3 routed surface items, locked in internal/providers/sqlddl/dw_integration_test.go (TestDB050_AdventureWorksDW_NoFalseAffirmation_RoutesToSurfaceInstead). Separately, the SAME investigation found and CLOSED a related but distinct defect: a non-single-space ADD/CONSTRAINT (e.g. \"ADD  CONSTRAINT\", two spaces) used to hit the generic \"ADD \" column branch and FABRICATE a phantom column/key literally named \"CONSTRAINT\" rather than dropping cleanly — this fabrication path is now recognized at its source (leadingKeyword) and converted into a recorded drop (MarkUnproven) exactly like the three shapes above; it does not gain new parsing support, it stops inventing data. Locked in internal/providers/sqlddl/fabrication_test.go. (7) NARROWED as of index-method-capture, 2026-07-31: reCreateIndex now DOES parse an anonymous PostgreSQL index (CREATE INDEX ON t (...), no index name — PostgreSQL generates the name) and T-SQL's ordinary CREATE [UNIQUE] [CLUSTERED|NONCLUSTERED] INDEX form (T-SQL's everyday standalone index syntax, not an exotic shape). SEPARATELY — a DIFFERENT symbol, not reCreateIndex widened — reCreateColumnstoreIndex now parses T-SQL's CREATE [CLUSTERED] COLUMNSTORE INDEX, on its own dedicated branch, since that specific shape carries no column list at all. All three now populate db.Index.Method (see Reasoning, above) instead of dropping the statement. What STILL is NOT parsed, and still marks its table unproven exactly as described below: PostgreSQL's ON ONLY clause on a partitioned parent table's own index; the standalone CREATE FULLTEXT/SPATIAL/XML/PRIMARY XML INDEX statement forms (distinct from the inline/ADD shorthand this package already reads elsewhere); CREATE NONCLUSTERED COLUMNSTORE INDEX — unlike its CLUSTERED counterpart, a nonclustered columnstore index DOES carry an explicit column list, a materially different shape from the one this slice closed, and is deliberately left for a future slice rather than folded in here; and a FOURTH MySQL USING position — between the index name and ON (CREATE INDEX ix USING BTREE ON t (a)) — which MySQL's grammar also allows but reCreateIndex does not match, since it requires \"on\\s+\" right after the optional name group. Behavior on all these remaining shapes is CORRECT (the drop is NOT SILENT, same as (6) above): apply()'s default: branch recognizes the CREATE INDEX-shaped head and marks the table's structural completeness (db.Table.Complete=false, ADR 0034), attributing the drop to its named table when the statement's ON (or ON ONLY) clause resolves one, or to Schema.Unreduced when it does not (a wrong attribution is worse than none), and amplifies schema-wide through paradigm.Classification.Unprovable exactly as before — only this enumeration was previously incomplete (missing the fourth USING position). Locked in internal/providers/sqlddl/unrecognized_index_form_test.go (ON ONLY and the standalone FULLTEXT/SPATIAL/XML/PRIMARY XML forms), internal/providers/sqlddl/index_method_capture_test.go (the fourth MySQL USING position), and internal/providers/sqlddl/tsql_ordinary_index_completeness_test.go (the now-recognized T-SQL CLUSTERED/NONCLUSTERED form, deliberately flipped to its opposite, now-correct outcome).",
		"SQL-DDL dialect assumptions: MySQL parsing assumes ANSI_QUOTES is OFF (a bare \" is read as a string literal, not an identifier quote); the parser binds a single dialect per project at construction (a project mixing dialects is not supported). A project mixing .prisma and .sql schema inputs is likewise out of scope.",
	}
}
